{"name":"Mediaeval Gold","hex":"#ac7f48","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mediaeval","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Alternative spelling of medieval."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of medieval."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mediaeval"},{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Middle Ages","blocks":[{"text":"In the history of Europe, the Middle Ages or medieval period lasted approximately from the 5th to late 15th centuries, comparable with the post-classical period of global history. The medieval period is the middle epoch of the three traditional divisions of Western history: classical antiquity, the medieval period, and the modern period. The medieval period is itself subdivided into the Early, High, and Late Middle Ages."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Canterbury_Cathedral%2C_window_nXV_detail_%2846220634195%29.jpg/330px-Canterbury_Cathedral%2C_window_nXV_detail_%2846220634195%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Middle_Ages","wikidata":"Q12554"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Venice_city_scenes_-_in_St._Mark%27s_square_-_St_Mark%27s_Basilica_%2811002237996%29.jpg/500px-Venice_city_scenes_-_in_St._Mark%27s_square_-_St_Mark%27s_Basilica_%2811002237996%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Venice_city_scenes_-_in_St._Mark%27s_square_-_St_Mark%27s_Basilica_%2811002237996%29.jpg/1280px-Venice_city_scenes_-_in_St._Mark%27s_square_-_St_Mark%27s_Basilica_%2811002237996%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Venice_city_scenes_-_in_St._Mark%27s_square_-_St_Mark%27s_Basilica_%2811002237996%29.jpg/500px-Venice_city_scenes_-_in_St._Mark%27s_square_-_St_Mark%27s_Basilica_%2811002237996%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A late Roman sculpture depicting the four Tetrarchs, now in Venice, Italy[21]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVenice_city_scenes_-_in_St._Mark's_square_-_St_Mark's_Basilica_(11002237996).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Europe_and_the_Near_East_at_476_AD.png/500px-Europe_and_the_Near_East_at_476_AD.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/comm